25115966796875, 0.00983428955078125, -0.0251007080078125, -0.01885986328125, 0.054931640625, 0.0143585205078125, 0.0086517333984375, 0.0159149169921875, 0.005035400390625, 0.0230255126953125, 0.0118560791015625, -0.0181427001953125, -0.026123046875, 0.00647735595703125, 0.018524169921875, 0.004184722900390625, 0.003704071044921875, -0.007343292236328125, -0.02593994140625, -0.0059661865234375, 0.00951385498046875, 0.0031566619873046875, 0.002353668212890625, 0.004978179931640625, -0.00732421875, 0.0075531005859375, -0.028289794921875, 0.004364013671875, -0.00620269775390625, 0.027008056640625, 0.022918701171875, 0.0138702392578125, 0.002330780029296875, 0.009368896484375, 0.03448486328125, -0.00823974609375, 0.045501708984375, -0.022003173828125, 0.0237579345703125, 0.0210113525390625, -0.027496337890625, 0.0303192138671875, -0.0165863037109375, -0.00010645389556884766, -0.0010366439819335938, -0.04541015625, -0.0121307373046875, -0.00995635986328125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "District heating networks are pivotal for achieving low-carbon heat supply, but their efficient and reliable operation remains challenging, especially for systems with complex, meshed topologies, nonlinear hydraulic and thermal constraints, time-varying renewable sources, and integrated thermal storage. Traditional optimization approaches\u2014commonly mixed-integer nonlinear programming (MINLP) solvers and local, gradient-based methods\u2014often fail to efficiently address these challenges at scale. While much of the literature centers on steady-state optimization or dynamic simulation, scalable global optimization for large, realistic, multi-period district heating networks remains an open research challenge.\n\n**Objectives and Main Contributions:**  \nThis paper addresses the need for scalable, high-fidelity global optimization of district heating networks. Our main goals are: (i) to develop an improved network modeling framework that preserves both hydraulic and thermal nonlinearities, (ii) to introduce and detail algorithmic enhancements for global optimization tailored to large, meshed networks with storage and multi-period operation, and (iii) to systematically benchmark our methods against established MINLP and local optimization solvers. The principal contributions are as follows:  \n- Quantitative characterization of the steady-state approximation's applicability in district heating, with accessible criteria.\n- Development of specific branch-and-bound and symmetry-exploiting techniques for scalable optimization, with key steps summarized below.\n- Release of reproducible synthetic and real-world test sets, with detailed randomization protocol and full data/code access.\n- In-depth analysis of performance, uncertainties, and limitations, along with practical model selection guidance.\n\n**Modeling Framework and Assumptions:**  \nThe network optimization model employs a stationary, nonlinear formulation for each discrete time step (interpreted as a steady-state snapshot). This approach simplifies the representation of system dynamics while preserving essential nonlinearities and is justified when system transitions are slow relative to the selected time step\u2014a frequent condition for most operational and planning scenarios in district heating. Note, however, that in situations with rapid load shifting or short network time constants, fully dynamic models may be preferred, and results from this formulation may underestimate transient effects.\n\nCore governing equations:\n\n- **Hydraulic Equations:** The Darcy-Weisbach relation is used:\n  \\[\n  \\Delta p_{ij} = f_{ij}(Q_{ij}, T_{ij}) = \\lambda_{ij} \\frac{L_{ij}}{D_{ij}} \\frac{1}{2} \\rho(T_{ij}) \\left( \\frac{Q_{ij}}{A_{ij}} \\right)^2\n  \\]\n  Here, \\(\\Delta p_{ij}\\) is pipe pressure drop, \\(Q_{ij}\\) is volumetric flow, \\(T_{ij}\\) is average temperature, \\(\\lambda_{ij}\\) friction factor, \\(L_{ij}\\) length, \\(D_{ij}\\) diameter, \\(\\rho(T)\\) temperature-dependent density, and \\(A_{ij}\\) cross-sectional area. Empirical functions for \\(\\rho(T)\\) and heat capacity \\(c_p(T)\\) use cubic-polynomial fits of tabulated water properties (see Appendix Sec. S1). We enforce these detailed relations to capture temperature feedback in fluid properties, which is critical for operationally relevant optimization.\n\n- **Thermal Energy Balances:** At each node:\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{\\text{inlets}} \\dot{m}_{\\ell}^{\\text{in}} h(T_{\\ell}^{\\text{in}}) = \\sum_{\\text{outlets}} \\dot{m}_{m}^{\\text{out}} h(T_{m}^{\\text{out}}) + Q_{\\text{demand}, k}\n  \\]\n  with \\(\\dot{m}\\) the mass flow, \\(h(T) = c_p(T) \\cdot T\\) the specific enthalpy, and \\(Q_{\\text{demand},k}\\) the thermal demand at node \\(k\\). To define unique mixing temperatures at nodes with multiple inlets, enthalpy-weighted blending is used:\n  \\[\n  T_{\\text{mix}} = \\frac{\\sum_i \\dot{m}_i T_i}{\\sum_i \\dot{m}_i}\n  \\]\n  when \\(c_p(T)\\) is approximately constant near \\(T_{\\text{mix}}\\). This ensures physical consistency in temperature assignment at mixing points.\n\n- **Operational Bounds:** At every time step, lower and upper limits for mass flow rates (\\(\\dot{m}_{\\min}, \\dot{m}_{\\max}\\)), pipe temperatures (\\(T_{\\min}, T_{\\max}\\)), and node pressures are enforced in accordance with equipment ratings and safe operation. Parameter choices and units are provided in Table S1; these bounds prevent unphysical or hazardous solutions and guide practical deployment of results. Users should be aware that overly narrow bounds can overconstrain the problem, while loose bounds might result in solutions that are unfeasible in practice.\n\n**Steady-State Assumption\u2014Quantitative Applicability:**  \nHydraulic and thermal timescales characterize the network's response to disturbances. Hydraulic settling time (\\(\\tau_h = V_{\\text{net}} / \\dot{V}_{\\text{main}}\\)) governs mass flow propagation, while thermal timescale (\\(\\tau_t = L_{\\text{max}} / v_{\\text{flow}}\\)) reflects how quickly temperature changes travel through the network. For example, consider \\(V_{\\text{net}} = 20,000\\) m\u00b3 and \\(\\dot{V}_{\\text{main}} = 1\\) m\u00b3/s: \\(\\tau_h = 20,000\\) s (\\(\\approx 5.5\\) h). For \\(L_{\\text{max}} = 5000\\) m and \\(v_{\\text{flow}} = 1\\) m/s, \\(\\tau_t = 5000\\) s (\\(\\approx 1.4\\) h). According to [3] and industry data, if the simulation time step \\(\\Delta t\\) is at least an order of magnitude larger than the dominant timescale (i.e., \\(\\Delta t \\geq 10 \\tau_h\\)), steady-state errors typically remain below 2%. In cases with fast-changing demands\u2014such as small-diameter pipes (\\(D < 50\\) mm) or rapid load variations (>40% change/hour)\u2014the assumption may not hold, and steady-state models may underpredict dynamic constraints, leading to errors above 10%. Users are advised to (1) compute \\(\\tau_h\\) and \\(\\tau_t\\) as above; (2) compare \\(\\Delta t\\) to \\(10 \\tau_h\\); (3) review if planned analyses entail scenarios with high-frequency load fluctuations. Example checks and a parameter lookup table are presented in Table S2.\n\n**Algorithmic Methodology:**  \nTo demonstrate and evaluate our modeling innovations, we developed and tested the following algorithmic frameworks focusing on both scalability and generalization to realistic network structures. The applicability of these techniques is strongest for meshed, moderately sparse thermal networks but could require adaptation for highly irregular or dynamic topologies; future research will assess their robustness for such cases. Summarized below are the primary methods and their justifications:\n\n1. **Cyclic Flow Exclusion:**  \n   - *Motivation:* In meshed networks, enforcing that net flow around cycles is zero eliminates redundant degrees of freedom and infeasible cyclic assignments, thus reducing the optimization search space.\n   - *Steps:* (a) Use Paton\u2019s algorithm to detect all fundamental cycles in the network graph; (b) For each, select a dependent flow variable and express it using Kirchhoff\u2019s law in terms of the others; (c) Remove one hydraulic variable per cycle. (See Algorithm S1.)\n\n2. **Supply-Return Symmetry Exploitation:**  \n   - *Motivation:* Many networks contain structurally identical subnetworks or consumer branches; identifying these allows pruning of equivalent solution branches during global search, resulting in computational savings.\n   - *Steps:* (a) Identify isomorphic subnetworks with a graph automorphism algorithm such as NAUTY [4]; (b) Canonically label nodes and edges; (c) During branch-and-bound search, eliminate redundant solution branches corresponding to symmetric configurations.\n\n3. **Temperature Mixing Constraint Reduction:**  \n   - *Motivation:* Redundant nodal temperature mixing constraints can unnecessarily expand the optimization constraint set, slowing solver progress.\n   - *Steps:* (a) Generate the full mixing-equation matrix; (b) Use Gaussian elimination to remove linearly dependent constraints; (c) Retain only the set necessary to ensure physical mixing behavior. (See Algorithm S2.)\n\nTogether, these enhancements reduce the number of branch-and-bound tree nodes that need to be explored by more than 50% on average (see Table 1 and Appendix Table S3), directly contributing to improved solver tractability. These methods are tested on a diverse set of networks, but their application to more complex or dynamic district heating layouts remains an area for further study.\n\n**Primal Heuristics:**  \nTo make practical use of our optimization infrastructure\u2014and to enable a fair and transparent comparison with established solvers\u2014we employ two principal heuristics. First, we use a linearized version of the full nonlinear model (neglecting certain temperature/pressure dependencies) to produce an initial feasible solution that jump-starts the nonlinear solver's convergence. Second, we refine these solutions using an iterative projection method: the current primal solution is adjusted to more closely satisfy the original nonlinear constraints, guided by the Jacobian (the matrix of constraint derivatives), and simultaneously moving along the negative cost gradient (the direction that reduces the objective function). This approach quickly identifies promising solution regions for subsequent global search.\n\nBranching priorities\u2014i.e., variable rankings in search tree exploration\u2014are established using the relaxed solution, which is obtained by temporarily ignoring integrality or some nonlinear constraints, and ranking variables based on their impact on temperature"}}